  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nModel soundings show plenty of moisture today with precipitable\nwater values of 1.6 to 2 inches. Effective layer CAPE is \ngenerally in the 1200 to 1800 range. Those values combined with\nstorm motions around or under 10 knots could lead to the \npotential for flash flooding this afternoon and evening. \n\nSome drier air does move into northern West Virginia and central\nOhio on Wednesday. Plenty of moisture remains further south \nhowever, with precipitable water values still in the 1.75 to 2 \ninch range over northeastern Kentucky, central and southern West\nVirginia, and southwest Virginia. With the ground already \nsaturated in many locations, can't rule out some localized flash\nflooding in those locations. \n\nWith chances of showers and thunderstorms through the week and \nsome areas becoming saturated, localized flash flooding \ncontinues to be a possibility Thursday into the weekend.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMoisture remains over much of the area through the work week \nand into the next weekend. This will allow for showers and \nthunderstorms each day. The precipitation will be more diurnally\ndriven through Thursday. A cold front and upper air disturbances\nwill then increase chances outside of the typical afternoon and\nevening hours for Friday into the weekend. \n\nSome dry air does slip in on Wednesday over central Ohio and\nnorthern West Virginia, possibly limiting convection there.\nFurther south the dry air is mainly in the mid levels with plenty\nof moisture remains in the lower levels. This could lead to \nsome stronger storms producing damaging winds Wednesday \nafternoon and evening over southern West Virginia, northeast \nKentucky, and southwestern Virginia.",
